    duplicate_of: []  # 1847
    not_duplicate_of: []  # 1847
    pr_ref:  # 1847
    description: "**Origin.** Filed 2026-05-30 from the unlinked-investigation G7 sub-agent (notebookjournal_05302026_0411_unlinked_G7_ux.md latent-META-1). Three dogfood items document the same regression class: help text and README claims drift from current CLI behavior with no enforcement gate.\n\n**The shape.** Hypergumbo's user-facing surface has two sources of truth: argparse-defined CLI behavior (in `cli.py` and subcommand modules) and the README/docs. The two sources are independently edited; nothing in CI asserts they agree. Symptoms:\n\n1. **`--debug` help text references removed ripgrep functionality.** The help string still describes a feature that was removed; the flag is now a no-op for that context. (INV-bugiz)\n\n2. **`routes --help` still documents the `-x/--exclude-tests` flag.** Tests are excluded by default; the flag is a no-op kept only for argparse backward compatibility. The help text doesn't say so. (WI-loloz)\n\n3. **`--no-progress` is accepted only by `sketch` and `run`, but the README implies it's general.** README:139 lists `--no-progress` as a top-level option; in practice the other subcommands reject it. (WI-figor)\n\n**Shared root: no spec-text-vs-behavior gate.** No CI smoke test diffs `--help` output against a removed-feature denylist, and no doctest harvests README code-blocks and confirms they execute. Every removed feature is a candidate to leave stale text; every README change is unanchored.\n\n**Members at filing time.** INV-bugiz (P3), WI-loloz (P4), WI-figor (P2).\n\n**Fix shape (cheap).** Add a CI smoke test: `pytest tests/test_help_text_drift.py` that (a) iterates every subcommand, runs `<subcmd> --help`, greps for a denylist of removed-feature substrings (ripgrep, exclude-tests-as-active-flag, etc.), and fails if any match; (b) parses README code-blocks of the form ` ```bash\\nhypergumbo ...\\n``` ` and confirms each invocation parses (`--help`-equivalent check). Cost: ~50 lines of test code.\n\n**Adjacent.** This META overlaps with INV-fabov (silent input acceptance) in that both surface gaps stem from absent input-text validators. They are distinct enough to file separately: INV-fabov is about runtime argument values not being validated; this META is about static spec-text not being validated against behavior."  # 1847
    fields:  # 1847
      statement: "Every spec-text claim (CLI --help string, README invocation example) must agree with current argparse-defined behavior; no removed-feature reference and no false-scope claim may persist in user-facing text."  # 1847
      root_cause: "No CI gate diffs --help output against a removed-feature denylist; no doctest harvests README code-blocks. CLI behavior and spec text are independently edited; drift is the default. Symptoms: --debug help references removed ripgrep functionality (INV-bugiz); routes --help still documents the -x/--exclude-tests flag which is now a no-op (WI-loloz); README:139 implies --no-progress is general but only sketch/run accept it (WI-figor)."  # 1847
- op: discuss  # 6a87
  at: '2026-06-02T07:10:16Z'  # 6a87
  by: agent  # 6a87
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 6a87
  clock: 2  # 6a87
  nonce: 6a87  # 6a87
  message: "Pass-30 face: subcommand flag-parity drift — `--input` accepted by most subcommands but rejected by `config`; `verify-claims` uses `--claims` instead (F30.15).\n\nINV-rotup catalogues 3 prior examples (INV-bugiz --debug stale, WI-loloz routes -x stale, WI-figor --no-progress only sketch/run). Pass-30 finds a 4th instance — but at the flag-AVAILABILITY axis rather than the flag-DOCUMENTATION axis:\n\n**Observation.** Most subcommands accept `--input <bm.json>` to consume a substrate file (sketch, run, slice, search, routes, explain, symbols, test-coverage, dead-code-maybe, io-boundaries, verify-claims, compact). Two divergences:\n1. **`hypergumbo config --input <bm.json>`** rejects with `error: unrecognized arguments: --input`. So `config` subcommand cannot consume a cached substrate; it must re-discover from path. (Tested: `config --format json` works without --input; with --input it fails.)\n2. **`hypergumbo verify-claims --claims <path>`** requires `--claims` (not `--claims-file`) for the claims-spec input. The flag naming differs from the typical `--<file-purpose>` pattern used elsewhere.\n\nThe first is a producer-design choice (config is path-driven, not substrate-driven) but consumers reading `--help` won't infer this without checking — many would try `--input` and get the unrecognized-arguments error. The second is a minor flag-naming nit (the obvious guess based on common CLI conventions is `--claims-file`).\n\n**Severity.** Low. Affects discoverability for consumers writing scripts against the CLI.\n\n**Fix-path candidates.** Either:\n(a) `config` accepts `--input` and ignores it (no-op), to make flag accept-set uniform.\n(b) `config --help` explicitly notes `--input` is unsupported.\n(c) Or canonicalize on `--claims-file` for verify-claims.\n\nSame root pattern as WI-figor's \"--no-progress accepted only by sketch/run\" — a flag exists on subset of subcommands without spec-level documentation of which. Cross-subcommand flag-availability matrix would be the systematic fix.\n\nNo status change. Pass-30 supplies the 4th instance to INV-rotup's catalogued symptoms.\n\nProbe artifact: pass-30 probe 3 CLI testing — `config --format json --input ...` rejected with unrecognized-arguments error."  # 6a87
- op: discuss  # 3219
  at: '2026-06-02T17:13:29Z'  # 3219
  by: agent  # 3219
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 3219
  clock: 3  # 3219
  nonce: '3219'  # 3219
  message: "Pass-34 F34.D3 — `hypergumbo search` has split case-sensitivity policy that is undocumented.\n\nThe positional `pattern` argument is case-INSENSITIVE — documented in `search --help`: \"Pattern to search for (case-insensitive substring match)\". This holds: pattern `User` matches `user_id` etc.\n\nThe `--language` and `--kind` flag values are case-SENSITIVE — NOT documented. Tested (`/tmp/p34_outputD.log` D.5):\n- `--language python` → 5 results\n- `--language PYTHON` → 0 results\n- `--language Python` → 0 results\n- `--kind function` → 5 results\n- `--kind FILE` → 0 results (canonical `file` kind matches uppercase substring `FILE` in pattern but not as --kind value)\n\nTwo case-sensitivity policies in the same command, both undocumented. INV-rotup's existing scope is \"CLI help text and README claims drift from current behavior, no enforcement.\" Pass-34 adds a new symptom class: undocumented case-sensitivity asymmetry. The user's mental model from --help is \"case-insensitive substring match\" — they then apply that to flag values and get silent zero-result, no error, no hint.\n\nCousin to pass-32 F32.6 (`hypergumbo config klingon` silent-accept) — both are flag-validation gaps but at different layers: F32.6 is registry-membership (INV-murig), F34.D3 is case-normalization asymmetry (INV-rotup scope). Fix-path: either case-fold flag values at parse time, or document the case-sensitive behavior in --help.\n\n**Probe artifact:** `/tmp/p34_outputD.log` D.5 (5 language values tested, only lowercase canonical matches). No status change."  # 3219
